# Line-ending policy
* text=auto eol=lf
*.vbs binary
#
# CRITICAL: Windows batch files MUST keep CRLF endings.
# cmd.exe mis-tracks its parse offset on LF-only batch files and silently eats
# bytes -- `chcp` becomes `cher.`, `pwsh` becomes `user` -- which surfaces as a
# cascade of "is not recognized as an internal or external command" errors.
# This bug shipped once already; do not let git normalise it back to LF.
#
# PowerShell tolerates LF, but these are Windows launchers and are kept CRLF so
# no tool has to guess.  Run app\check_launchers.ps1 to verify.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
*.psd1          text eol=crlf

# Shell launchers for non-Windows use must stay LF.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Source and docs may use the platform default; git decides.
*.py            text
*.js            text
*.css           text
*.html          text
*.json          text
*.md            text

# Never touch binary-ish assets.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.webp          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.mp4           binary
*.mkv           binary
*.zip           binary
*.tgz           binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
